"Selectable" meant selectable on the AWS path only
==================================================
Date : 2026-07-29
Mode : real uvicorn webhook + real analyzer (Bedrock), live Alertmanager payloads
Gate : make check 1454 -> 1470 (+16)


Where this came from
---------------------
Yesterday's live run of the executor-span work surfaced it as a side observation:
an on-prem disk-filling alert resolved to `generic-recovery` even though a
`disk-full` runbook had just been made selectable. It was recorded as a matching
*design decision*. It was not — three separate wiring defects were stacked on top
of each other, and each one had to be removed before the next became visible.


Defect 1 — `reason` was a copy of `metric_name`
-------------------------------------------------
`_synthetic_alarm` built the AlarmContext for every non-AWS signal with

    reason      = incident.signal_type
    metric_name = incident.signal_type      # the very next line

Runbook matching scores keywords over `f"{metric_name} {reason} {root_cause}"`,
so it was reading "availability availability …". The alert name and summary had
been normalised and stored the whole time (`source_metadata` / `observations`)
and simply never reached selection.

Fixed provider-neutrally: each adapter names the rule differently (Alertmanager
`alertname`, GCP `policy_name`/`condition_name`, Azure `alert_rule`), so the keys
are looked up rather than special-cased, and `signal_type` remains the fallback
so an adapter carrying none of them is no worse off than before.


Defect 2 — `resource_types` was declared everywhere and read by nothing
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Widening the matcher's text raises the chance of a keyword hit on a runbook that
cannot apply. That turned out to be unguarded: `resource_types` is on every
runbook, parsed into the dataclass and serialised back out, and selection never
consulted it. Nothing stopped an RDS runbook being chosen for a Kubernetes
workload — and the failure is quiet, because `_resolve_runbook_actions` catches
the unresolvable capability and falls back to the runbook's **hardcoded AWS
action names**, handing the executor actions for the wrong provider.

Gate added, fail-open on either side being unknown. generic-recovery is never
gated — it is the answer for "nothing fits", which is what makes the gate safe.

That is the fifth "declared, valid, unconsumed" field found in two days.


Defect 3 — a stale seed capped what could ever be selected
------------------------------------------------------------
With 1 and 2 fixed the unit tests passed and the **live webhook still disagreed**:
`KubePodNotReady` selected `eks-pod-oom`. Scored by hand:

    eks-pod-oom            score=1  hits=['OOM']            [in seeded table]
    health-check-failure   score=3  hits=['readiness',
                                          'notready','probe'] [built-in only]

The weaker match won because its catalog was scanned first. Yesterday's fix
(`allow_generic=False`) stopped the stale table answering when *nothing*
matched; it did nothing about the table answering with a *worse* match. Two
heuristics over two catalogs are now one heuristic over the union, with scanned
rows scored first so they still win ties. Tier 1 — the exact `alarm_name`
operator override — is untouched. → `DECISIONS` D35.


And a defect in my own test
-----------------------------
The first version of the on-prem cases passed while the live webhook still
answered generic-recovery for the same alert. The summaries I wrote contained
the runbook's own keyword ("…, disk full projected in 18h"); a real
Alertmanager alert says `NodeFilesystemSpaceFillingUp` and "78% used", which hit
nothing. The test was fitted to the keyword list rather than to what
Alertmanager sends.

Rewritten with realistic alert text containing no literal keyword phrase, and
the catalogs given stems grounded in standard kube-prometheus-stack names
(`filesystem`, `outofspace`, `spacefilling`, `notready`, `probe`) alongside the
existing CloudWatch metric names. Matching is substring and case-insensitive, so
a stem catches the CamelCase name without hardcoding it.


Live result (same webhook, same payload shapes as the failing run)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
    NodeFilesystemSpaceFillingUp   -> disk-full             [ONPREM-CleanupDiskSpace, ONPREM-ExpandVolume]
    KubePodNotReady                -> health-check-failure  [ONPREM-RolloutRestartWorkload, ONPREM-ArgoRolloutRollback]
    CertManagerCertExpirySoon      -> certificate-expiry    [ONPREM-RenewCertificate, ONPREM-CreateChangeRequest]
    KubePodCrashLooping            -> eks-pod-oom           [ONPREM-RolloutRestartWorkload, ONPREM-ScaleWorkload]

Every action resolves to an ONPREM-* action, which is also the proof that no
wrong-provider fallback fired. The last row is the regression check: it selected
`eks-pod-oom` before this change and still does.


Falsification
--------------
  - `reason` reverted to signal_type            -> 4 failures
  - resource-type gate removed                  -> 1 failure
  - tiers reverted to sequential (scan first)   -> 6 failures
Restoring passed all 36.


WHAT IS STILL OPEN
-------------------
- **The deployed DynamoDB table still holds only the five original rows.**
  Selection no longer depends on that, but an operator wanting to override the
  four newer runbooks has nothing to edit until the next deploy re-seeds
  `builtin_runbook_items()`. Deploy action, not a code change.
- Keyword matching remains a scoring heuristic over text. It is now fed the
  right text and bounded by resource type, but it is not classification — an
  alert whose name and summary share no vocabulary with any runbook still lands
  on generic-recovery, which is the correct answer for "nothing fits".
- Verified against on-prem and AWS-shaped signals. GCP/Azure `reason` now draws
  from `policy_name`/`condition_name`/`alert_rule`, unit-covered but not
  exercised against a live GCP/Azure alert payload.
